-- title:
--   §3–§4, pp. 5–11, 20–24 — ϵ-DeGroot random action process, extremization rules, centrality, agreement, information structure, η-naive learning
-- statement:
--   This file sets up the random action model of Arieli, Babichenko and Mueller-Frank.
--
--   **Network.** A set $V=\{1,\dots,m\}$ of agents carries a weight matrix $W=(w_{ij})$. It is a **weight matrix of a strongly connected network** when it is row-stochastic ($w_{ij}\ge 0$, $\sum_j w_{ij}=1$), irreducible (the directed graph with an edge $i\to j$ whenever $w_{ij}>0$ is strongly connected), and $w_{ii}>0$ for every agent $i$. A **centrality measure** $\mu=\mu^W$ is a probability vector with $\mu W=\mu$, i.e. a stationary distribution of $W$.
--
--   **Random action process.** Fix a decision rule $\sigma$, an inertia parameter $\epsilon$ and an initial belief vector $\mathbf x^1$. Independent uniform coins $U_i^t\in[0,1]$ (one per agent and period) generate actions $a_i^t=\mathbf 1[U_i^t<\sigma(x_i^t)]$, so that agent $i$ plays action $1$ with probability $\sigma(x_i^t)$, independently across agents and periods. Beliefs follow the $\epsilon$-DeGroot recursion (1):
--   $$
--   \mathbf x^{t+1}=(1-\epsilon)\,\mathbf x^t+\epsilon\, W\mathbf a^t .
--   $$
--   The coin filtration $\mathcal F_t$ is generated by the coins of the first $t$ periods.
--
--   **Agreement.** For given $(\sigma,\epsilon,W,\mathbf x^1)$, agreement holds when, with probability one, there are a time $t^*$ and an action $a^*\in\{0,1\}$ with $a_i^t=a^*$ for every agent and every $t\ge t^*$ (Definition 1).
--
--   **Extremization family.** For a prior $p$, a family $(\sigma_\delta)_{\delta\in(0,1]}$ is an extremization family if each $\sigma_\delta$ maps $[0,1]$ into $[0,1]$, is Lipschitz on $[0,1]$, satisfies $\sigma_\delta(x)>x$ for $x\in(p,1)$ and $\sigma_\delta(x)<x$ for $x\in(0,p)$, and $\sigma_\delta\to\mathrm{id}$ uniformly on $[0,1]$ as $\delta\to0^+$ (p. 7).
--
--   **Information structure.** The state $\omega\in\{0,1\}$ has prior $p=\Pr[\omega=1]$; given $\omega$, the agents' signals are i.i.d. with law $F_\omega$, and the coins are independent of state and signals. An agent's first belief is the Bayesian posterior
--   $$
--   x^1_i=\Pr[\omega=1\mid s_i]=\frac{d(pF_1)}{d(pF_1+(1-p)F_0)}(s_i).
--   $$
--
--   **Naive learning.** The process satisfies $\eta$-naive learning if, with probability at least $1-\eta$ under the joint law of state, signals and coins, there is a time $t^*$ from which every agent plays $a_i^t=\omega$ (Definition 2). The number $l^G_F=p\,\Pr(\sum_i\mu_ix^1_i>p\mid\omega=1)+(1-p)\Pr(\sum_i\mu_ix^1_i<p\mid\omega=0)$ is the one of p. 24.
--
--   **Mean-field ODE.** The mean field of the $\sigma$-DeGroot process is $z\mapsto W\vec\sigma(z)-z$, the right side of (10) (and of (6)–(7) for $\sigma=\mathrm{id}$); a forward solution on $[0,\infty)$ is a continuous path with this right derivative at every $t\ge0$.
--
--   These objects are shared by every statement of the mission.
--
--   **Formalization Note** Lean time $t=0$ is the paper's period $1$: `beliefAct σ ε W x1 U 0 = x1`, and `action … t i` is the action of period $t+1$. Beliefs live in `Fin m → ℝ` (the sup norm), the coins in `ℕ → Fin m → unitInterval` under the infinite product of uniform laws, and the full sample space is `(Bool × (Fin m → S)) × Coins m` with `true` for state $1$. The posterior is a Radon–Nikodym derivative, defined up to a null set. The centrality vector is carried as data with its defining properties (it is unique under irreducibility). In the mean field, each coordinate is projected onto $[0,1]$ before $\sigma$ is applied; this changes nothing on $[0,1]^m$ and makes the field globally Lipschitz. Property 3 of p. 7 is pointwise; it is strengthened here to uniform convergence, which the proof of Lemma 4 (p. 22) uses and which the paper's examples satisfy.
-- source:
--   Arieli, Babichenko & Mueller-Frank, Naive Learning Through Probability Over-Matching, SSRN working paper 3338015 (version of August 3, 2020), pp. 5–11, §3, (1), Definitions 1–3, vanishing influence p. 11; pp. 20–24, (6), (7), (10), l^G_F p. 24

import Mathlib

namespace NaiveLearn.OverMatch

open MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory Filter Topology

/-- §3, pp. 5–6: a weight matrix of a strongly connected network. `W` is row-stochastic,
irreducible (its positive-entry graph is strongly connected, fn. 7 p. 5, fn. 9 p. 6) and has a
positive diagonal (`w_ii > 0` for every agent, p. 6). -/
def IsWeightMatrix {m : ℕ} (W : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ) : Prop :=
  W ∈ Matrix.rowStochastic ℝ (Fin m) ∧ W.IsIrreducible ∧ ∀ i, 0 < W i i

/-- The centrality measure `µ^W` (p. 6): a probability vector with `µ W = µ`, i.e. the
stationary distribution of `W` (unique when `W` is irreducible). -/
def IsCentrality {m : ℕ} (W : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ) (μ : Fin m → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ i, 0 ≤ μ i) ∧ ∑ i, μ i = 1 ∧ Matrix.vecMul μ W = μ

/-- Action coins: `U t i ∈ [0, 1]` is agent `i`'s coin at Lean time `t`. -/
abbrev Coins (m : ℕ) := ℕ → Fin m → unitInterval

/-- The law of the coins: all `U t i` independent and uniform on `[0, 1]`. -/
noncomputable def coinLaw (m : ℕ) : Measure (Coins m) :=
  Measure.infinitePi (fun _ : ℕ => Measure.pi (fun _ : Fin m => (volume : Measure unitInterval)))

/-- The action played by an agent with belief `y` and coin `u` under the decision rule `σ`:
`1` if `u < σ y`, otherwise `0`. With `u` uniform this is `1` with probability `σ y`. -/
noncomputable def actOf (σ : ℝ → ℝ) (u : unitInterval) (y : ℝ) : ℝ :=
  if (u : ℝ) < σ y then 1 else 0

/-- The belief process of the random `ε`-DeGroot process with decision rule `σ`, recursion (1),
p. 6: `x^{t+1} = (1 − ε) x^t + ε W a^t`. Lean time `0` is the paper's period `1`, so
`beliefAct σ ε W x1 U 0 = x1` is the initial belief `x¹`. -/
noncomputable def beliefAct {m : ℕ} (σ : ℝ → ℝ) (ε : ℝ) (W : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ)
    (x1 : Fin m → ℝ) (U : Coins m) : ℕ → (Fin m → ℝ)
  | 0 => x1
  | t + 1 =>
      (1 - ε) • beliefAct σ ε W x1 U t +
        ε • W.mulVec (fun j => actOf σ (U t j) (beliefAct σ ε W x1 U t j))

/-- The action `a_i^t` of agent `i` at Lean time `t` (the paper's period `t + 1`). -/
noncomputable def action {m : ℕ} (σ : ℝ → ℝ) (ε : ℝ) (W : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ)
    (x1 : Fin m → ℝ) (U : Coins m) (t : ℕ) (i : Fin m) : ℝ :=
  actOf σ (U t i) (beliefAct σ ε W x1 U t i)

/-- The coin filtration: `𝓕_t` is generated by the coins `U_0, …, U_{t−1}`, i.e. by everything
that determines `x^0, …, x^t`. -/
def coinFiltration (m : ℕ) : Filtration ℕ (MeasurableSpace.pi : MeasurableSpace (Coins m)) where
  seq t := MeasurableSpace.comap (fun (U : Coins m) (s : Fin t) => U s) inferInstance
  mono' t t' h := by
    have hc : (fun (U : Coins m) (s : Fin t) => U s) =
        (fun (v : Fin t' → Fin m → unitInterval) (s : Fin t) => v (Fin.castLE h s)) ∘
          (fun (U : Coins m) (s : Fin t') => U s) := rfl
    simp only
    rw [hc, ← MeasurableSpace.comap_comp]
    exact MeasurableSpace.comap_mono
      (measurable_pi_lambda _ (fun s => measurable_pi_apply (Fin.castLE h s))).comap_le
  le' t := (measurable_pi_lambda (fun (U : Coins m) (s : Fin t) => U s)
    (fun s => measurable_pi_apply (s : ℕ))).comap_le

/-- Agreement for one `(ε, W, x¹)` (the property in Definition 1, p. 9): almost surely there are
a time `t*` and an action `a* ∈ {0, 1}` with `a_i^t = a*` for every agent and every `t ≥ t*`. -/
def EventualAgreement {m : ℕ} (σ : ℝ → ℝ) (ε : ℝ) (W : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ)
    (x1 : Fin m → ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∀ᵐ U ∂(coinLaw m), ∃ t0 : ℕ, ∃ a : ℝ, (a = 0 ∨ a = 1) ∧
    ∀ t ≥ t0, ∀ i, action σ ε W x1 U t i = a

/-- An extremization family `δ ↦ σ_δ` for the prior `p` (p. 7). For every `δ ∈ (0, 1]`, `σ_δ`
maps `[0, 1]` into `[0, 1]`, is Lipschitz on `[0, 1]`, satisfies `σ_δ(x) > x` on `(p, 1)` and
`σ_δ(x) < x` on `(0, p)`; and `σ_δ → id` uniformly on `[0, 1]` as `δ → 0⁺` (property 3, read
uniformly as in the proof of Lemma 4, p. 22). -/
def IsExtremizationFamily (p : ℝ) (σ : ℝ → ℝ → ℝ) : Prop :=
  (∀ δ ∈ Set.Ioc (0 : ℝ) 1,
      Set.MapsTo (σ δ) (Set.Icc 0 1) (Set.Icc 0 1) ∧
      (∃ K, LipschitzOnWith K (σ δ) (Set.Icc 0 1)) ∧
      (∀ x ∈ Set.Ioo p 1, x < σ δ x) ∧
      (∀ x ∈ Set.Ioo 0 p, σ δ x < x)) ∧
  TendstoUniformlyOn σ id (𝓝[>] 0) (Set.Icc 0 1)

/-- The first-period (Bayesian) belief of an agent with signal `s` under the information
structure `(p, F₀, F₁)`: `Pr[ω = 1 | s] = d(p F₁) / d(p F₁ + (1 − p) F₀) (s)`. -/
noncomputable def posterior {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] (p : ℝ) (F0 F1 : Measure S)
    (s : S) : ℝ :=
  ((ENNReal.ofReal p • F1).rnDeriv
    (ENNReal.ofReal p • F1 + ENNReal.ofReal (1 - p) • F0) s).toReal

/-- The joint law of the state, the signals and the coins on
`(Bool × (Fin m → S)) × Coins m`: `ω = true` (state 1) with probability `p`; given `ω`, the
signals are i.i.d. with law `F_ω`; the coins are i.i.d. uniform and independent of both. -/
noncomputable def fullLaw {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] (m : ℕ) (p : ℝ) (F0 F1 : Measure S) :
    Measure ((Bool × (Fin m → S)) × Coins m) :=
  ((ENNReal.ofReal p • (Measure.dirac true).prod (Measure.pi fun _ : Fin m => F1)) +
    (ENNReal.ofReal (1 - p) • (Measure.dirac false).prod (Measure.pi fun _ : Fin m => F0))).prod
    (coinLaw m)

/-- The learning event of Definition 2, p. 10: there is a time `t*` from which every agent plays
the realized state (`1` if `ω = true`, `0` if `ω = false`); the initial beliefs are the
posteriors of the agents' signals. -/
def LearnEvent {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] {m : ℕ} (σ : ℝ → ℝ) (ε p : ℝ)
    (W : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ) (F0 F1 : Measure S) : Set ((Bool × (Fin m → S)) × Coins m) :=
  {ξ | ∃ t0 : ℕ, ∀ t ≥ t0, ∀ i,
      action σ ε W (fun j => posterior p F0 F1 (ξ.1.2 j)) ξ.2 t i = if ξ.1.1 then 1 else 0}

/-- `η`-naive learning (Definition 2, p. 10): the learning event has probability at least
`1 − η`. -/
def NaiveLearning {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] {m : ℕ} (η : ℝ) (σ : ℝ → ℝ) (ε p : ℝ)
    (W : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ) (F0 F1 : Measure S) : Prop :=
  1 - η ≤ ((fullLaw m p F0 F1) (LearnEvent σ ε p W F0 F1)).toReal

/-- The number `l^G_F` of p. 24: `p P(Σ µ_i x¹_i > p | ω = 1) + (1 − p) P(Σ µ_i x¹_i < p | ω = 0)`,
with `x¹_i` the posterior of agent `i`'s signal. -/
noncomputable def lGF {S : Type*} [MeasurableSpace S] {m : ℕ} (p : ℝ) (F0 F1 : Measure S)
    (μ : Fin m → ℝ) : ℝ :=
  p * ((Measure.pi fun _ : Fin m => F1)
      {s | p < ∑ i, μ i * posterior p F0 F1 (s i)}).toReal +
  (1 - p) * ((Measure.pi fun _ : Fin m => F0)
      {s | ∑ i, μ i * posterior p F0 F1 (s i) < p}).toReal

/-- `z` is a forward solution of `ż = f(z)` on `[0, ∞)`. -/
def IsODESolution {E : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup E] [NormedSpace ℝ E] (f : E → E)
    (z : ℝ → E) : Prop :=
  ContinuousOn z (Set.Ici 0) ∧ ∀ t : ℝ, 0 ≤ t → HasDerivWithinAt z (f (z t)) (Set.Ici t) t

/-- The mean field of the `σ`-DeGroot process: `z ↦ W σ⃗(z) − z`, the right side of (10), p. 22
(and of (6)–(7), p. 20, for `σ = id`). Each coordinate is projected onto `[0, 1]` before `σ` is
applied, which changes nothing on `[0, 1]^m`. -/
noncomputable def meanField {m : ℕ} (σ : ℝ → ℝ) (W : Matrix (Fin m) (Fin m) ℝ)
    (z : Fin m → ℝ) : Fin m → ℝ :=
  W.mulVec (fun j => σ (Set.projIcc (0 : ℝ) 1 zero_le_one (z j))) - z

end NaiveLearn.OverMatch


